text = "At last, after so much peril and sorrow, Alice stumbled upon a looking-glass shining faintly between two twisted oaks. Her heart leapt—home! She pressed her palms to the glass, and it yielded, rippling like water, until she tumbled through and landed once more in her familiar room.
The air was calm, the familiar tick of the clock steady and kind. No shadows stretched too far, no whispers followed her steps. Only the gentle rustle of curtains, and the smell of hearth and home.
“Mother? Father?” she called, her voice trembling. And there they were—just as they had always been—waiting with smiles and open arms. Alice rushed into their embrace, and laughter, real laughter, filled the room once more.
Soon, she was seated at the table, sipping warm tea, the taste of biscuits crumbling sweetly on her tongue. The world outside the window seemed bright and steady, as if no nightmare could ever touch it.
Yet as she leaned back with a sigh of relief, something slipped from the folds of her dress. A playing card, torn and weathered, fluttered to the floor. It was the Queen of Hearts—split in two.
Alice stared for a moment, then tucked it quietly away. Some doors, she thought, are better left closed.
"
autowrap_mode = 3

